\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N$, $\mathbb Z$, $\mathbb Q$, $\mathbb R$, and $\mathbb C$ denote the natural numbers, integers, rationals, reals, and complex numbers, respectively. All additional parameters and hypotheses are specified in the question.


\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For an elliptic curve $E$ over a number field $K$, do the parity conjectures hold: assuming finiteness of the Tate–Shafarevich group, is $(-1)^{\operatorname{rank}E(K)}=w(E/K)$, and, for every prime $p$, is the corresponding $p$-Selmer-rank parity equal to $w(E/K)$? The stronger version asks for the rank-parity equality without assuming finiteness of the Tate–Shafarevich group.
\par\smallskip\textit{Formulation references:} \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}, \href{https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2007/34}{[S2]}.

\subsection{Short English statement}
